  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nAs of 1am radar indicates spotty rain showers in association with \nweak energy/moisture aloft over portions of south central SD. These \nwill continue to dissipate as they track east/northeast. Current \ntemps range in the 60s to around 70 and dewpoints in lower to mid \n60s with winds between 5-10mph.\n\nThe Northern Plains remains in slight southwest flow aloft with a \nridge overhead that will amplify a bit through the day with weak \nembedded shortwave pulses within it. At the surface winds will \ncontinue out of east this morning, switching to more southeasterly \nby the midday, as a weak low pressure system tracks over the MT/ND \nborder by the midday. In tandem, a lee low develops along the \nboundary over western/southwestern SD. With this setup, central SD \nwill be the focus for WAA showers and thunderstorms this morning and \nthrough the day, with the majority of the NBM pops (50-85%) over \nnorth central SD. Another potential area of showers and \nthunderstorms will be clipping east central SD (NBM pops of 20-30%) \nper another embedded shortwave tracking northeast from NE through IA \nthis afternoon and evening. Looking at the CAMS, some of these show \nsome daytime heating induced shower or perhaps weak thunderstorms \nacross northeastern SD/MN, so went ahead and added in slight chances \nto err on the side of caution. Late this evening through the \novernight hours, the center of the low will continue to track \neastward/northeast across ND (perhaps weakening into a trough) before \nstrengthening again over northeastern ND/Canada by 12Z Saturday and \nits cold front extending southward through the James River per HREF. \nThe low that was over western SD will track eastward as well and \nover south central SD by 12Z. Therefore, we will continue with a \nchance of showers and thunderstorms mainly across central SD to \nportions of northeastern SD/MN through early Saturday morning. So \nkept with the NBM which does a good job broadbrushing the pop \nchances due to the variability between CAMS as it keeps them more \nspotty with differences in location, timing, and intensity of \nsuggested precip with really any lack of LLJ to sustain them for \nvery long.\n\nHREF indicates marginal instability as CAPE values of 500-1000j/kg \nover the CWA are forecast this afternoon and evening, highest over \nsouth central SD, with values decreasing with sunset and bulk shear \nof 30kts. Along with weak lapse rates, severe weather is not \nanticipated at this time with just the threat of a weak thunderstorm \nor two if can grow vertically enough into the cooler air aloft. \nHowever PWAT values continue to range between 1.3-1.8\" so any \nstronger rain shower/storm or slow moving could produce heavy \nrainfall.\n\nThe center of the low will track northeast through Saturday and into \nQuebec by the evening with the cold front by then extending from MN, \nthrough southeastern SD into NE by 00Z Sunday. As mentioned, pops\nare possible over central SD Saturday morning, however, low confidence\nas CAMS do not show much in the way of precip beyond this time,\nwith much of it south and southeast of the CWA, as we really \nlose that moisture aloft as high pressure will filter into the \nCWA behind it. This high will keep the region dry Sunday with \nflow aloft turning zonal. A slight northwestern component aloft \nover the CWA returns early next as a weak ridge develops over \nthe western CONUS (then flattens out as it tracks a bit east) \nwith the Northern Plains between this and another low/trough \nmoving in over the Hudson Bay. Weak embedded shortwaves aloft \ninteracting with weak surface boundaries along with daytime \nheating/instability will bring chances of rain/storms each \nevening into the nighttime hours Sunday and Monday. More \nwidespread pops are possible Tuesday/Tuesday night per stronger \ncold front. Low confidence in details as this is several days \nout.\n\nWith a cooler airmass aloft, high temps will continue to be near to \nbelow average, ranging in the 70s to the lower 80s today through the \nweekend. Other than a bit of a warmup Monday, mainly west river, \ntemps will continue to be on the cooler side at least through the \nmidweek. Lastly, HRRR indicates smoke aloft will continue today, \nwith the higher concentrations tracking north and east this \nafternoon and evening with little to no concentrations of smoke over \nthe CWA late tonight through Saturday as winds aloft will be out of \nthe southwest, as mentioned.",
